                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Pea (Unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whatever you add</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Your own</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vegan smoothies</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Figures vary by flavor, bag size and label revision, so check the panel on the product you actually receive. Caffeine numbers for the ready-made powders come from the brand or retailer listings; the DIY picks contain none until you add matcha, and a level teaspoon of matcha typically adds somewhere around 40 to 90 mg depending on the grade, which we unpack in the caffeine section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Best Matcha Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These six are ranked on whether the matcha is real, protein per serving, stated caffeine, sweetener and value, from labels and listings at the time of writing rather than our own kitchen tests. Three are ready-made matcha blends and three are bases to add your own matcha to, because for many buyers that is the better buy. Figures vary by bag size and label revision.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="real-matcha-vs-flavoring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Real Matcha or Just Green Tea Flavor? How to Tell
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most matcha protein powders fall into one of two camps, and the ingredient list tells you which in about five seconds. The first camp lists matcha itself: "organic matcha green tea powder", "matcha (Camellia sinensis) leaf powder" or similar. That is the whole, stone-ground tea leaf, and it brings the real thing with it: caffeine, L-theanine, catechins such as EGCG, and the grassy, slightly bitter taste matcha drinkers actually want.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second camp lists "natural flavors", "green tea flavor" or "matcha flavor", often with a green color such as spirulina extract or a dye further down. These products taste vaguely of green tea and look the part, but they may contain little or no tea leaf. If caffeine is not stated anywhere on the label, that is a strong hint there is not much matcha in the tub, because real matcha cannot be decaffeinated without stripping its flavor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where matcha sits in the list matters</h3>

          <p>
            Ingredients are listed by weight, heaviest first. When matcha appears second or third, right after the protein, it is doing real work. When it appears after salt, gums and sweeteners, it is in the "contains 2% or less" zone and is there mostly for the front-of-pack claim. A 30 g scoop with matcha below the 2% line contains at most around half a gram of tea, compared with the 1 to 2 g you would whisk into a normal cup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both NOW and Truvani list matcha high in the ingredients and state caffeine, which is why they lead the ready-made picks. Vital Proteins goes further and lists the matcha dose, about 2 g per scoop, on the supplement facts panel. That level of disclosure is rare in flavored protein and worth rewarding with your money.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot tell from the listing, do the DIY route instead. A scoop of <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder</a> plus a teaspoon of matcha you chose yourself removes the guesswork entirely, and it is often cheaper per serving than a pre-mixed matcha protein because you are not paying a premium for the flavor system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="caffeine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Caffeine Is in Matcha Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less than most people fear and more than some expect. Published analyses of commercial matcha have found roughly 19 to 44 mg of caffeine per gram of powder, with higher grades made from young shade-grown leaves usually toward the top of that range. A level teaspoon is about 2 g, so a homemade matcha protein drink typically carries somewhere around 40 to 90 mg of caffeine. For comparison, an 8 oz cup of brewed coffee is usually around 95 mg.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ready-made matcha proteins sit lower because they use less matcha per scoop. Retailer listings put NOW's whey with matcha at roughly 32 mg per scoop, Truvani says its plant protein carries about 20 to 27 mg depending on the batch, and Vital Proteins' Matcha Collagen label states about 50 mg. Those are figures from the brands and listings at the time of writing; because matcha is a farm product, caffeine varies from harvest to harvest, which is why some brands give a range.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drink</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical caffeine</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Whey with Matcha (1 scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 32 mg (listings)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truvani Matcha Plant Protein (1 scoop)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20-27 mg (brand)</td>
                  </tr>
